        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Sorting living things"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A forest has trees, birds, insects, fungi and bacteria. To study millions of organisms, scientists sort them into groups by common features."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "Monera and Protista",
          "acellular entities",
          "biodiversity hotspot",
          "criteria",
          "five kingdoms",
          "fossils",
          "need for classification",
          "other kingdoms"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "India is a biodiversity hotspot, a region with a very large variety of species.\nWe classify organisms because a system of groups makes it easy to study, name and compare them.\nClassification uses features such as cell type, body organisation and mode of nutrition.\nThe five kingdoms are Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia.\nMonera are prokaryotes like bacteria. Protista are unicellular eukaryotes like Amoeba.\nFungi are eukaryotes that absorb nutrients and have cell walls; Plantae make food by photosynthesis; Animalia are multicellular consumers without cell walls.\nFossils are preserved remains of ancient organisms and are evidence of how life has changed over time.\nSome entities, like viruses, are acellular and do not fit neatly into the five kingdoms."
        }
